  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An active weather pattern will continue into \nthe weekend as an upper trough digs into the eastern CONUS and a\nweakening cold front approaches from the northwest. \n\nScattered thunderstorms continue to blossom to the west and\nnorth early this evening as the upper trough and sfc front \ncontinues to slowly move toward the area. An iso stronger storm\nwith gusty winds will be possible over the next few hours, but\nthreat continues to wane with loss of heating and instability. \nPW values remain high with 2.2\" observed in the 12z/28 KMHX \nsounding and stronger storms that develop will be capable of \nproducing heavy rainfall rates bringing the threat for localized\nflooding and WPC continues a slight risk (level 2 of 4) for \nexcessive rainfall across the region. \n\nThe upper trough and attendant front move into the area\novernight with the threat for showers and thunderstorms\ncontinuing but do not anticipate severe storms as instability\nand shear wanes. Continued unsettled on Saturday but the upper\ntrough begins to lift north of the area with height rises aloft\nand weakening dynamics throughout the day bringing a minimal\nthreat for severe thunderstorms but there remains a marginal \nrisk (1 of 4) for excessive rainfall. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper ridging builds into the region from the \nwest for the early to mid portion of next week bringing lower \nchances for showers and thunderstorms although cannot rule out\nisolated storms along the sea breeze through peak heating. The \nmain story will be the building heat with highs expected to \nclimb into the 90s early next week and peaking in the mid 90s as\nwe move into the middle of the week with heat headlines \npossible, Wednesday, Thursday and possibly lingering into Friday \nwith heat index values in the range of 105-115deg possible.",
